Here, we assign the address of a variable to the pointer.[code format="cpp" codeId="223"]int score = 5;
int *scorePtr;
[b]scorePtr = &score;[/b]

cout << scorePtr << endl;
[/code]
The code above declares a pointer to an integer called [b]scorePtr[/b], and assigns to it the memory location of the [b]score [/b]variable using the ampersand (address-of) operator.
[note]Now, [b]scorePtr's [/b]value is the memory location of [b]score[/b].[/note]

